A||||10||-=shame=-||Dog-bone , motor mounts||||5||
Z||000000||-=shame=-||08-24-2000||08:02 AM||||Well, here we go. I've seen some adds for polyurethane bushings for the dog bones. I can turn them at work for less $. I just need to know how do they fit? Real tight a little snug? Or if someone has dimensions that would be great ( my partner at work said to make them out of teflon but that puts me back up around the store bought poly)||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-24-2000||10:51 AM||||Teflon would be too hard anyway. You need some give in it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||-=shame=-||08-25-2000||08:14 AM||||So, I won't use Teflon, it would hold up to the heat better but you're right, it is too rigid. But I've found that I can get the poly for free so I still need to know what to expect for a fit. Please.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-25-2000||12:22 PM||||They are just snug in the aluminum. You need to find a sleeve for the core. the width of the installed bushings fill the brackets enough to keep the from falling out of the eyes. They have enough shoulder to keep the eye centered. <P>I'm not pulling mine out to measure it. sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hrocker||08-26-2000||06:39 AM||||I just got the poly bushings for my v-6 bone, but it has the oval in the inside so I have to die-grind it out.<P>Here's the dimensions of the poly bushings:<P>Height: 26.5 mm<BR>Smaller diameter where it fits into the dogbone: 45.5 mm<BR>Diameter of lip: 52.5 mm<BR>Lip thickness: 7 mm<BR>Center bore for sleeve/bolt: 16 mm<p>[This message has been edited by hrocker (edited 08-26-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Haze_Performance||08-26-2000||07:19 AM||||I have my stock one you can have. Its in great shape. It has 37,000 miles on it. It should make a nice sample to work from. The only catch is you'll need to pay the shipping. Ill ship it COD if you want it.<P>Let me know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycarsig.jpg">  <B>My 1985 2M4 Sport Coupe </B><P><BR><B>My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>H.M.P. </A></B>||||reg||14
Z||000006||the need for speed||08-26-2000||01:28 PM||||hrocker were did you get your poly bushings at.The rubber in my dog bone are pretty bad,I would like to keep the dog bone but just put some poly bushings in it,do they sell the bushings seperate and do they slid in the dog bone easy.or do you think it is easier to by a new one?||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-26-2000||02:21 PM||||hrocker, you need to get the old style strut. The new style one will likely break after you take out the taper. The taper is a big chunk of the strength of the eye. Worse, it's most likely to fail when you really need it most.<P>The old strut eyes are ribbed around the outside.||||reg||1
Z||000008||-=shame=-||08-28-2000||08:17 AM||||Hrocker, THANK YOU! I'll have 'em today, even get the poly for free. I swear this is the greatest place to work in the world. Guy across the parking lot details cars, buddy down the street makes poly deadblow hammers. This little shop can do just about anything. Last week we bored valve releifs in two big V8 .. don't know the actual size. <BR>Oh and Hazey ,, no thank you sir.<BR>Ogre ,, what will fail? the dimensions don't say anything about a taper?||||reg||6
Z||000009||theogre||08-28-2000||09:22 AM||||He's trying to stuff the polly into a new style strut. That strut is tapered inside. Unlike the old style one that is just a big round hole.||||reg||1
Z||000010||hrocker||08-28-2000||02:58 PM||||I got the bushings from the Fiero Store. I bought this car in February to restore, and since it's an '86 I didn't double check to see if the dogbone had the ridge on it which supposedly means it's a newer style strut, which it is. Yes, it would be way too thin after grinding the taper out. Haze, if that offer is good for an old style bone, I'll definitely take it!||||reg||14
